  "body": "The conjecture misread the rule. The rule's words are: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". The conjecture treats a four-backtick block as a code fence, but the check's own `_FENCE` regex defines a code fence as exactly three backticks (with optional `json`/`JSON` tag). Four backticks are not a code fence under this rule, so Reply A has no recognized fence and the last top-level object is `{\"b\": 2}`. The code's return of `{\"b\": 2}` for both replies is therefore consistent with the rule as written; the rule and code do not part company. A next conjecture should test a triple-backtick fence containing an object followed by another top-level object, to see whether the fenced object is chosen. It should avoid assuming Markdown code fence semantics and avoid using four backticks as a fence.",
